read:selfplay_actors_scale.summary:
Crucible keeps policies separate from world mechanics. Every participant acts only through public tools with the same permissions, prices and rate limits, and joins through the recorded operator call World.system_call("admin.join", {"handle","endowment","kind"}). After that it uses World.observe / visible_events / available_tools / execute and the clock moves with World.advance_time.

Independently controlled participants already living inside an OpenWorld 4.0 world such as ow_market:
- 4,000 vectorised numpy consumers in several behaviour segments;
- 20 Tier-2 "minds" (actors/minds.py): 6 firms, 2 compute providers and 12 entrepreneur/forum-reader persons. They have goals, beliefs, Beta trust, memory, negotiation and adoption;
- 14 prospective founders (actors/entry.py);
- 12 NPC workers and 1 lender (actors/npcs.py);
- NPC innovators and producer routines in other families.
The optional Tier-3 LLM minds (actors/llm_minds.py, params.llm_minds={"backend":"hf:Qwen/Qwen3-4B"|"test:accept","fraction":f}) are off by default. They only draft replies, which are clamped by the Tier-2 reservation logic.

External learners plug in at four levels:
(1) the agents.base.Policy protocol (act(obs, tools)->{"tool","args"}|None), driven by Runner (synchronous rounds) or AsyncRunner (independent clocks, plus a batch_group.act_batch hook for one batched forward pass), next to scripted Builder/Employer/Composer/RandomPolicy seats or LLMPolicy;
(2) training/league.py League (3.0 text-economy self-play: Elo/TrueSkill, PFSP, held-out population; matches run sequentially in one process);
(3) selfplay/ (4.0): SelfPlayEnv/KernelSelfPlayEnv/Individual/SelfPlayLeague. These handle numeric hidden-mechanism "rig" facilities only, behind the research.learner.Learner contract (61-d observation, 12-d action);
(4) Gymnasium CrucibleEnv / PettingZoo CrucibleParallelEnv in crucible/api, the HTTP/MCP servers, and the scale/rollout.py WorldEnv semi-Markov wrapper.

Multiprocess infrastructure exists. scale/pool.py WorldPool runs declarative world jobs (isolation, deterministic digests, checkpoints, worker recycling). scale/rollout.py RolloutFarm runs asynchronous rollout workers with versioned parameter blobs, backpressure and persistent world keys routed to the same worker. Both share Supervisor (spawn start, default cap of 8 workers, pid-budget check). Neither has a central cross-process inference server: the policy is built inside each worker.

Experience formats: crucible-traj/1 JSONL/NPZ from training/trajectories.py (RecordingRunner, ImitationDataset), crucible-events/1, the Rollout dataclass, and selfplay and league JSON. Traces are an append-only, hash-chained event log in the Memory/SQLite/Postgres stores, with snapshots, fork lineage and anchors, plus analytics.trace timeline/explain/causal_chain and metrics_export (JSON/Prometheus).

The dashboard (dashboard/app.py) is a read-only FastAPI + Jinja2 app bound to loopback. It already serves world, society, markets, agents, trajectories, evaluation, trace and experiments pages, using reusable section kinds (cards/table/svg/pre/form/links) and SVG helpers. It has no learner-side views (checkpoints, gradients, world-model calibration, imagined-vs-real), so Part 18 needs new routes or a new experiment format.

Rewards come from goals/contracts.py RewardContract: potential shaping on verified metrics plus a success bonus, from GoalContract/GoalVerifier. Self-play Objective is own/joint/score/mixed. League scores use independent revenue. WorldEnv's default reward is the change in own cash.

Security: only code an agent submits through tools (code.run, cloud.deploy, web apps) runs in the sandbox. That sandbox drops to uid nobody, applies Landlock and seccomp (no socket/exec/fork), sets rlimits, gives an empty environment and allows only numpy; I verified this with a probe here. The learner's own process, the kernel and trusted genesis code run unsandboxed. The synthetic internet simply has no sockets.

This host has kernel 5.15, so Landlock is ABI 1 and the Landlock network layer the docs claim is absent; seccomp alone blocks sockets.

Parallelism here is bounded by the CPU quota of 23.04 cores (not the 48 logical CPUs). Memory (limit about 85 GiB) and pids (5,888) are not binding. Each world process costs about 51 MB baseline plus about 2.5-3 MB per simulated day of in-memory event log (unbounded up to log_retain=2M events), plus about 15 MB per sandboxed service worker. One ow_market world is created in about 40 ms and about 0.5 MB.

